#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Refuse a run whose must-fire artefacts did not fire.

    check_fired.py RUNLOG [--require NAME ...]
                          [--declared TASK_YAML --variant VARIANT]

WHY THIS EXISTS
---------------
A control that passes is not evidence the clause holds. It is evidence the
control did not run, and THE TWO ARE INDISTINGUISHABLE IN THE OUTPUT.

Four instances in one week. THIS TOOL COVERS TWO OF THEM, and the header said
three until the coverage was measured -- which is the error the tool exists to
find, made in the tool's own documentation. The measurement is below.

  COVERED -- 1. v_ca06's first A5 negative control PASSED. It needed three
     consecutive stalled cycles; backpressure is aperiodic and `m_arvalid`
     enters the antecedent 18 times in a whole run, so it never triggered.

         A5 control, threshold 3:  RESULT: PASS   FIRED ...drop 0   -> REFUSED
         A5 control, threshold 2:  RESULT: FAIL   FIRED ...drop 5   -> OK

     The passing run is refused and names the reason. This is the instance the
     tool was built for and it catches it.

  COVERED -- 2. v_ca03's read-coverage floor sat behind a dangling `else` and
     ran only on a run that had ALREADY failed -- skipped exactly when the run
     was otherwise clean, which is the only situation a floor exists for. A
     floor that emits its count unconditionally cannot be skipped silently:
     absent is refused as loudly as zero.

  NOT COVERED -- 3. v_ca06's `dwc_c1` and `dwc_c5` declared themselves slow on
     the downstream response path, and a double drive left `g_rready` connected
     to nothing, so that channel was never slow. Measured in the historical
     configuration -- the pre-repair perturbation against the pre-A5 testbench,
     the run in which it passed:

         FIRED dwc_c1.r_backpressure 5      -> OK, and the channel was inert

     Five, not zero. The double-driven `m_rready` did go low sometimes; it just
     was not the gate doing it. A port-level count answers "did this channel
     stall" and the question needed was "did THIS PERTURBATION stall it", which
     no counter on the perturbation can answer, because a bypassed mechanism
     produces no counter at all. What finds that is a DIFFERENTIAL check -- a
     perturbation must differ from the golden somewhere observable, or it is
     inert -- and that is a different instrument. NON-ZERO IS NOT A FLOOR.

  NOT COVERED -- 4. A clause the reference satisfies only on the fast path,
     because the task never leaves the fast path (v_ca06's D6). Its antecedent
     IS entered; what is unexplored is the timing space around it. That needs a
     sweep over the reference at settings nobody chose.

Saying which two is part of the job: a checker that implies more coverage than
it has is the defect it was built to find.

A FIFTH INSTANCE, AND A SUB-SHAPE THE CONVENTION HAS TO NAME EXPLICITLY
----------------------------------------------------------------------
From AGENT-DESIGN-43a92055, on d_ca01's M3:

    chk(m3_overlap_err == 0, "a memory request was issued while a"
                             " transaction was in flight");

`m3_overlap_err == 0` is VACUOUSLY TRUE for a design that issues no memory
transaction at all. The check cannot separate "never issued two at once" from
"never issued one".

A never-happens assertion counts nothing by construction -- its whole form is an
absence -- so "did it fire" is not the right question and a tool keying on
"a counter exists and reads zero" sees nothing, because there is no counter.

THE RULE THAT MAKES IT COVERED: for a never-happens assertion, the FIRED count
is NOT how often the assertion fired. It is HOW OFTEN THE SITUATION IT FORBIDS
WAS REACHABLE -- for M3, the number of memory transactions issued at all. That
is a DIFFERENT counter from the one the assertion uses, and it has to be written
deliberately; nothing derives it from the assertion. d_ca01 now counts `m3_txns`
and gates on non-zero, reference unchanged at 16/16.

So the convention below applies to four kinds, and the fourth is the one that
has to be said out loud:

    a control              -> how many times its trigger asserted
    a coverage floor       -> the count it already keeps
    a perturbation's gate  -> how many cycles it was closed on a live offer
    a NEVER-HAPPENS check  -> how many times its antecedent was REACHABLE

THE CONVENTION
--------------
Any artefact whose evidentiary value depends on it triggering prints, once, at
the end of a run:

    FIRED <name> <count>

A control, a coverage floor, a perturbation's gate, a checker's antecedent. The
count is how many times the trigger occurred. A verdict says what happened; this
says whether anything was asked.

WHY IT REFUSES AND DOES NOT WARN
--------------------------------
Rule 24: a control must refuse, not warn. Every artefact above was GREEN while
broken. A warning in the same stream as the run's normal output is exactly the
channel that failed for the combinational loop this week -- Verilator reported
it as UNOPTFLAT and it arrived among thirty warnings about the vendored anchor
under -Wno-fatal, which is the correct setting and made the new warning
invisible by dilution. A permanently noisy channel is not a channel.

ABSENT AND ZERO ARE DIFFERENT FAILURES, and are reported differently. Zero means
the artefact ran and its trigger never occurred. Absent means the artefact is
not in this run at all -- deleted, renamed, or never reached -- and no count can
be inferred from silence (rule 20).

A DUPLICATE NAME IS A REFUSAL, not a sum. Two artefacts reporting under one name
is the clause-grouping shape: one of them can be at zero while the other carries
the line, and the reader sees a healthy number. If two things fire, they get two
names.
"""
import re

# LEADING WHITESPACE IS ALLOWED, AND THE ANCHOR USED TO BE COLUMN 0.
# Every regex in this file was `^`-anchored and every one of them was correct
# only because its input happens to start at column 0 -- Verilator emits
# warnings there, `$display` emits there, markdown tables and headings sit
# there. That is a property of the INPUT, not of the method.
#
# It cost two rows in a corpus sweep: a `task_text_hash:` field indented by two
# and another by four read as ABSENT, which is a legitimate state 14 tasks
# occupy, so the miss landed in a plausible bucket instead of an implausible
# one. AGENT-DESIGN-43a92055's parser had the same defect and their remedy is
# the one taken here: not care, but A CASE LIST THAT FAILS WHEN THE SCOPE
# NARROWS. The self-test below carries indented and tab-indented forms.

import sys

LINE = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*FIRED\s+([A-Za-z_][\w.:-]*)\s+(\d+)\s*$", re.M)


def scan(text):
    """-> (counts, duplicates). Duplicates are names seen more than once."""
    counts, dup = {}, []
    for name, n in LINE.findall(text):
        if name in counts:
            dup.append(name)
        counts[name] = int(n)
    return counts, sorted(set(dup))


def check(text, required=()):
    counts, dup = scan(text)
    zero = sorted(n for n, c in counts.items() if c == 0)
    absent = sorted(n for n in required if n not in counts)
    return counts, dup, zero, absent


def main(argv):
    if not argv or argv[0] in ("-h", "--help"):
        sys.exit(__doc__.strip().splitlines()[0])
    log, required = argv[0], []
    declared = variant = None
    declared_variant = False
    i = 1
    while i < len(argv):
        if argv[i] == "--require":
            required.append(argv[i + 1]); i += 2
        elif argv[i] == "--declared":
            declared = argv[i + 1]; i += 2
        elif argv[i] == "--variant":
            variant = argv[i + 1]; i += 2
        else:
            sys.exit("unknown argument %r" % argv[i])
    # DECLARED PER VARIANT, NOT PER TASK. A flat per-task list refuses on every
    # run that does not contain every artefact -- the negative-control run has no
    # conformant perturbations in it and vice versa -- and a gate that fires on
    # runs it does not apply to is one that gets routed around within a week.
    if declared:
        if not variant:
            sys.exit("--declared needs --variant: which run is this?")
        y = open(declared, encoding="utf-8").read()
        blk = re.search(r"^must_fire:\s*$(.*?)(?=^\S|\Z)", y, re.M | re.S)
        if blk:
            row = re.search(r"^\s+%s:\s*\[([^\]]*)\]" % re.escape(variant), blk.group(1), re.M)
            declared_variant = True
            if row is None:
                print("REFUSED: %s declares must_fire but not for variant %r." % (declared, variant))
                print("  An undeclared variant is not an exempt one. Add an empty")
                print("  list to say deliberately that nothing must fire in it.")
                return 2
            required += [r.strip() for r in row.group(1).split(",") if r.strip()]
    counts, dup, zero, absent = check(open(log, encoding="utf-8").read(), required)

    if not counts and not required:
        # AN EXPLICIT EMPTY LIST IS NOT SILENCE. `reference: []` is a task saying
        # deliberately that nothing here must fire -- which is itself a finding
        # worth being able to state -- and it must not be reported as the same
        # thing as a task that declared nothing at all.
        if declared_variant:
            print("  (nothing declared for this variant, deliberately)")
            print("OK: the declaration is an explicit empty list. Read the task's")
            print("  own note for why -- an empty list is a claim, not an absence.")
            return 0
        print("REFUSED: no FIRED lines in %s, and nothing was required." % log)
        print("  A run that declares nothing about what fired cannot be checked,")
        print("  and reporting that as OK would be the defect this tool is for.")
        return 2
    for n in sorted(counts):
        print("  %-44s %s%s" % (n, counts[n], "   <-- NEVER FIRED" if counts[n] == 0 else ""))
    rc = 0
    if dup:
        print("REFUSED: reported twice under one name: %s" % ", ".join(dup))
        print("  Not summed. One of them can be at zero while the other carries")
        print("  the line, and the reader sees a healthy number.")
        rc = 2
    if zero:
        print("REFUSED: %d artefact(s) never fired: %s" % (len(zero), ", ".join(zero)))
        print("  Their verdicts carry no information about the clause they are for.")
        rc = 2
    if absent:
        print("REFUSED: %d required artefact(s) reported nothing: %s" % (len(absent), ", ".join(absent)))
        print("  ABSENT is not ZERO (rule 20): the artefact is not in this run at")
        print("  all -- deleted, renamed, or never reached -- and no count can be")
        print("  inferred from silence.")
        rc = 2
    if rc == 0:
        print("OK: %d artefact(s) declared a trigger and every one of them fired." % len(counts))
    return rc


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
